WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.540
Ordinary collections such as List, Dictionary, Queue, and Stack are not automatically safe for concurrent mutation.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:19.720
If multiple operations add, remove, enumerate, or update at the same time without a rule, the collection can produce inconsistent results or fail in hard-to-reproduce ways.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.240
System.Collections.Concurrent provides collection types designed for concurrent access patterns.

4
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:37.140
ConcurrentDictionary, ConcurrentQueue, ConcurrentStack, ConcurrentBag, and related types can reduce manual locking when their operation model matches the workflow.

5
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:42.040
They are useful tools, but they are not a replacement for understanding the full invariant.

6
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.640
A thread-safe collection makes individual collection operations safe according to that collection's contract.

7
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.980
It does not automatically make a multi-step application workflow correct.

8
00:00:53.080 --> 00:01:01.880
For example, checking for a value and then performing a separate business operation may still require coordination if another operation can change the relevant state between steps.

9
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.920
Producer-consumer designs separate work submission from work processing.

10
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.180
Producers add work items, and consumers process them.

11
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:16.680
This can improve control over throughput, retries, failure handling, and dependency pressure.

12
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:23.180
It also gives teams a place to define message shape, ownership, and operational visibility.

13
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.940
Queues should be bounded or backpressure-aware when producers can outpace consumers.

14
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:36.280
An unbounded queue can hide overload until memory, storage, latency, or downstream capacity becomes a problem.

15
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:43.020
Bounded designs force the system to make an explicit decision when demand exceeds safe processing capacity.

16
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.900
Immutable collections and snapshots can simplify reasoning where update patterns allow them.

17
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.920
Instead of sharing a mutable collection across readers and writers, a component can publish a stable snapshot and replace it when updates are ready.

18
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.060
That approach can reduce locking and make reader behavior easier to understand.
